WEBVTT

a01
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.153
So. By the end of this walkthrough you can take a microphone signal and turn it into text — and say what every number along the way actually means. Two bridges. Sound into a picture. Then a picture into letters, with nobody telling us where each letter begins.

a02
00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:30.231
Here is the raw audio. One number per measurement, sixteen thousand of them every second. That is it. That is the whole format. And look at that line — there is nothing in it that resembles a letter.

a03
00:00:30.581 --> 00:00:45.907
Speech does not sit still. It changes every few tens of milliseconds. So we chop it into short windows — 25 ms wide, moving only 10. Watch the box slide. Each window overlaps the last, so nothing falls between them.

a04
00:00:46.257 --> 00:01:01.959
One window is 400 samples. An FFT turns it into 201 numbers, one for each band of 40 Hz. So — that is the frequency content of a single moment, one hundredth of a second wide.

a05
00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:14.022
But 201 is more than we need. So 80 triangular filters squash them down to 80, and a logarithm compresses the loudness. And that. That is one column of the picture.

a06
00:01:14.372 --> 00:01:26.085
Now do it for every window, and stand the columns side by side. 100 columns every second. And sound has become a picture — time running across, frequency running up.

a07
00:01:26.435 --> 00:01:44.067
And here is the part that pays you back. You can read it. Strong energy low down? That is a vowel. A bright fuzzy block at the top with no stripes? A fricative, an s. Learn four shapes and you can debug an audio pipeline with your eyes.

a08
00:01:44.717 --> 00:02:02.830
But why 80 bands, and why placed where they are? Because your ear is not linear. 100 Hz to 200 Hz is an enormous change. 4000 to 4100? You can barely hear it. Same number of hertz. Nothing like the same difference.

a09
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:20.253
So we warp the axis. This is the mel scale. Space the 80 filter centres evenly along it — perfectly even — then map every centre back to real frequency. Watch the lines lean. The spacing crowds up at the left and stretches wide at the right.

a10
00:02:20.603 --> 00:02:37.043
Each centre becomes a triangular filter. Narrow and crowded low down, wide and sparse up high. About 43 of the 80 end up below 2 kHz. And the whole bank is one fixed matrix, so applying it is a single matrix multiply.

a11
00:02:37.693 --> 00:03:02.922
Right. Now the hard part. Five seconds of speech is 500 frames, and the transcript is a couple of dozen characters. So who labels which frame is which letter? Nobody. CTC answers with one extra symbol — the blank. And blank does not mean silence.

a12
00:03:03.272 --> 00:03:15.075
Rule one. Merge neighbours that carry the same symbol. So the two h frames at the start become one, and the pair of l frames become one too. That is all rule one does.

a13
00:03:15.425 --> 00:03:27.869
Rule two. Delete every blank. And look — ten frames have become five letters. Hello. Nobody ever told this model where the e begins. It worked that out on its own.

a14
00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:39.931
Now watch this one blank. The one sitting between the two l frames. It looks like nothing at all. It is doing real work — it holds those two apart, so rule one cannot merge them.

a15
00:03:40.281 --> 00:03:55.558
Take it away, and the word loses a letter. Hello becomes helo. That is the whole reason the blank symbol exists. It is a separator. Not a silence detector.

a16
00:03:56.208 --> 00:04:09.361
So if the model invents its own timing — which timing do we train it towards? Here is one path over eight frames, for the word "cat". It says the c lasted two frames, and the t lasted two more.

a17
00:04:09.711 --> 00:04:26.465
Here are two more paths. The letters land in different places, and blanks pad out the rest. Three completely different stories about when each sound happened. Now collapse all three. The differences vanish. Same word, every time.

a18
00:04:26.815 --> 00:04:40.167
For eight frames and the word "cat" there are 462 such paths. And CTC picks none of them. It adds up the probability of all 462, and training pushes that whole sum up.

a19
00:04:40.517 --> 00:05:01.018
The loss is minus the log of that sum. A forward algorithm gets it with dynamic programming, so 1000 frames cost about 1000 steps, not an astronomical number. In the lesson you run it by hand on three frames. Six paths, 0.510. Loss, 0.673.

a20
00:05:01.668 --> 00:05:17.379
Right. So CTC has a weakness. One symbol per frame, always — and no idea what it just wrote. The transducer fixes both, and it decodes on a grid. Frames run across. Emitted tokens run down.

a21
00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:35.481
Three networks meet on this grid. The encoder gives one vector per audio frame, along the top. The prediction network gives one per token written so far, down the side — that is a small language model. And a joint network mixes them at every node.

a22
00:05:35.831 --> 00:05:54.303
Two moves, and only two. Emit the blank and you step right, on to the next frame, writing nothing. Emit a token and you step down — writing one token, staying on the same frame. Watch. Frame two writes a c, then an a, without advancing at all.

a23
00:05:54.653 --> 00:06:06.076
Two blanks carry us across the quiet frames. A t comes out on frame four. One last blank finishes the audio — and the path has spelled the word.

a24
00:06:06.426 --> 00:06:22.208
Now take the down-moves away. What is left may only step right, one symbol per frame. That is CTC. And notice the path never goes backwards. That is monotonic — and it is why a transducer never waits for the end of a sentence.

a25
00:06:22.858 --> 00:06:34.931
So what does all this cost you in delay? Whisper always processes exactly 30 seconds. Your three-second command goes in, and 27 seconds of zeros are packed around it to fill the box.

a26
00:06:35.281 --> 00:06:47.310
Watch the meter fill. The encoder cost does not depend on how much you said. 1500 encoder states, every single time. So most of that work is spent on silence you invented yourself.

a27
00:06:47.660 --> 00:07:04.252
A streaming encoder does the opposite. Zoom into those same three seconds and feed it one small chunk at a time. It keeps its KV cache between chunks — the same trick as Lesson 04 — so each chunk pays only for its new frames.

a28
00:07:04.602 --> 00:07:19.195
And each chunk may peek a little way into the future. That is look-ahead. It buys you accuracy, and it costs exactly its own duration in delay. So your latency is the chunk, plus the look-ahead, plus the compute.

a29
00:07:19.545 --> 00:07:36.218
And here is the trade in real numbers. 80 ms chunks give 8.43 % word error. 1.12 s chunks give 6.93 %. Fourteen times the delay, for 1.5 points. Same weights. One runtime flag.

a30
00:07:36.868 --> 00:07:50.021
Last thing. How do you measure any of this honestly? Count the words in the reference — what the person actually said. There are nine. That nine is the denominator, and it never changes.

a31
00:07:50.371 --> 00:08:04.880
Line the output up against it by edit distance, not by position — otherwise one early slip marks everything after it wrong. And here: the word "to", before "book", never got written. That is one deletion.

a32
00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:15.007
Further along, "to" came back as "two". That is one substitution. And "today" was never said at all. That is one insertion.

a33
00:08:15.357 --> 00:08:36.649
Three errors over nine reference words. 33.3 %. And insertions are not capped by that nine, so WER can pass 100 %. One last thing: 8.4 down to 6.9 is 1.5 points absolute, but 18 % relative. Relative always sounds bigger.

a34
00:08:37.599 --> 00:09:00.041
That is the whole path. Air pressure to text. Now go and build it: your own features, a keyword model at about 95 % accuracy, then a real recogniser scored twice — once raw, once normalised. Lesson 12 runs this backwards, text to waveform. Lesson 13 makes it the ears of an assistant.

